course: dictionary
lesson: 6722
title: Words from sport
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to one game, and the rules explain them. Offside is a sports word. It describes a player who is in a place the rules don't allow, usually too far forward. «Sports rules make games fair: in soccer the offside rule stops attackers waiting by the goal.»
explain: Use the context to work out the meaning. The clues are the goal, the ball and the last defender. If an attacker is nearer the other team's goal than the ball and the second-last defender, they are offside. Being level with them is onside, which is allowed.
explain: The word also works in different ways. You can say «the offside rule» (a noun), «flagged offside» (the referee shows the rule was broken) or «be offside» (a verb phrase). Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have it.
fact: Offside means in a position the rules don't allow in sports such as football (soccer), rugby and hockey.
fact: In soccer, an attacker is offside if he is nearer the other goal than the ball and the second-last defender when a teammate passes forward.
fact: Being level is onside, which is allowed.
fact: «There is no offside straight from a corner kick, a throw-in or a goal kick.»
fact: In the UK, offside can also describe a car: the side nearest the middle of the road, which is the driver's side.
exercise: In sport, what does «offside» mean? | Scoring the winning goal | *In a position the rules don't allow, usually too far forward | Standing still on the grass
exercise: «There is no offside straight from a corner kick, a throw-in or a goal kick.» Which of these is a restart where there is no offside? | A car on the road | *A throw-in | A player standing level with the last defender
exercise: Ezra says offside is always a car word. Is he right? | Only in rugby | Yes, in every country and every sport | *No, in sport it means a player in a position the rules don't allow | Only at a bus stop
test: Why does the offside rule stop attackers waiting near the goal? | To make players tired | To stop the match ending | To give the goalkeeper more work | *To keep the game fair
test: A player is level with the second-last defender when the ball is passed forward. What is the result? | Off the pitch for a rest | Penalty kick | *Onside, so it is allowed | Offside, so the goal is cancelled
exam: In the phrase «the offside rule», what kind of word is offside? | A question word | *A noun | A verb | A full stop
